 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "My Hotpoint washer dryer isn't drying properly any more and there's a funny smell when it's on the dry bit. Is there something I should clean?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Before you start, switch it off and unplug it. Yes, you should clean the condenser unit and the lint filter. These components often trap moisture and debris that cause poor drying performance and unpleasant odors. From what you have described, the most likely cause is the drying heater or its thermostat. Next: Clean the lint/fluff filter and (on condenser models) the condenser, and make sure the airflow path isn't restricted. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, I'll unplug it. I'm not sure what a condenser is on mine, and I didn't know the fluff filter needed cleaning. The smell is like burning dust, and the top of the machine gets really hot. I've still been using it though. Where do I find the filter?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "⚠ Stop using this appliance\nA burning or hot-plastic smell, smoke, scorching or melting can mean an electrical fault or a fire risk. Stop using it, switch it off and unplug it (or turn it off at the fuse box), and don't run it again — not even as a test — until it has been checked by a qualified engineer. On most Hotpoint tumble dryers, the main lint filter is located at the bottom of the door opening or inside the door frame. You can pull it out to clean it before starting a cycle. From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
